5,5,5,5,5,1,5,5,5,5,5,1,5,5,5],[0,0,0,0,0,5,0,0,0,0,0,5,0,0,0,0,0,5,0,0,0,0,0,5,0,0,0],[0,0,0,0,0,5,0,0,0,0,0,5,0,0,0,0,0,5,0,0,0,0,0,5,0,0,0],[0,0,0,0,0,5,0,0,0,0,0,5,0,0,0,0,0,5,0,0,0,0,0,5,0,0,0],[0,0,0,0,0,5,0,0,0,0,0,5,0,0,0,0,0,5,0,0,0,0,0,5,0,0,0],[0,0,0,0,0,5,0,0,0,0,0,5,0,0,0,0,0,5,0,0,0,0,0,5,0,0,0],[5,5,5,5,5,1,5,5,5,5,5,1,5,5,5,5,5,1,5,5,5,5,5,1,5,5,5],[0,0,0,0,0,5,0,0,0,0,0,5,0,0,0,0,0,5,0,0,0,0,0,5,0,0,0],[0,0,0,0,0,5,0,0,0,0,0,5,0,0,0,0,0,5,0,0,0,0,0,5,0,0,0],[0,0,0,0,0,5,0,0,0,0,0,5,0,0,0,0,0,5,0,0,0,0,0,5,0,0,0],[0,0,0,0,0,5,0,0,0,0,0,5,0,0,0,0,0,5,0,0,0,0,0,5,0,0,0]]}]},"teaching_variant":"canonical","teacher_models":["claude-opus-5-5"],"explanation":"**What the grids are made of.** Each input is a lattice of full-length lines in one colour (5 in every pair) on a 0 background. A second \"noise\" colour (3, 2 or 4) is scattered over the grid. Some noise cells sit on the lines and replace a 5 there. Others sit in the empty cells between the lines.\n\n**What the output does.** Each output is the same lattice, but cleaned up:\n- every noise cell in the open cells becomes background;\n- every noise cell sitting on a line becomes the line colour again;\n- every crossing of a horizontal line with a vertical line becomes the noise colour.\n\nSo in pair 2, with lines at columns 4 and 9 and rows 4, 9 and 14, the six crossings turn to 2 and nothing else is left non-background.\n\n**How to find the lines.** Noise only ever replaces line cells with another non-background colour; it never punches a 0 into a line. That means a divider row or column is exactly one in which every cell is non-background. A random row of open cells is never completely filled by noise in these grids.\n\n**Colours.** The background is the most common colour. The line colour is the most common non-background colour. The noise colour is the remaining colour.\n\n**On the test.** The line colour is 5 and the noise colour is 1.\n- The full rows are 5, 11 and 17.\n- The full columns are 5, 11, 17 and 23.\n- The output is these lines in 5 on a clean 0 background, with the 12 crossings coloured 1.\n\n**Construction stages emitted:**\n1. A blank background canvas.\n2. The divider lines drawn in the line colour.\n3. The intersections marked with the noise colour.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    g = np.array(grid)\n    bg = background(g)\n    vals, counts = np.unique(g[g != bg], return_counts=True)\n    order = np.argsort(-counts)\n    line_c = int(vals[order[0]])\n    noise_c = int(vals[order[1]]) if len(vals) > 1 else line_c\n    rows = [y for y in range(g.shape[0]) if np.all(g[y] != bg)]\n    cols = [x for x in range(g.shape[1]) if np.all(g[:, x] != bg)]\n    out = np.full_like(g, bg)\n    emit(out, \"Clear everything to background; the noise and lattice will be redrawn cleanly.\")\n    for y in rows:\n        out[y, :] = line_c\n    for x in cols:\n        out[:, x] = line_c\n    emit(out, \"Redraw every full divider row/column (all non-background in input) in the line colour.\")\n    for y in rows:\n        for x in cols:\n            out[y, x] = noise_c\n    emit(out, \"Mark each line intersection with the noise colour.\")\n    return out\n","candidate_tactics":{"panels":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"That means a divider row or column is exactly one in which every cell is non-background."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"The divider lines drawn in the line colour."},{"source":"verified program checkpoint","text":"Redraw every full divider row/column (all non-background in input) in the line colour."}],"selection":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"**Colours.** The background is the most common colour."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"The line colour is the most common non-background colour."}],"boolean":[{"source":"verified program checkpoint","text":"Mark each line intersection with the noise colour."}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"7131f7d748831